Title: "Magmas and Monoids"
Id: "b66194ab-1d9d-4b6f-bf99-d68a98b4cc00"


Text: "Abstract algebra studies sets equipped with operations, asking what can
       be proved from the properties of the operations alone, regardless of what
       the elements are. This chapter climbs a ladder of such structures. Each
       rung adds one requirement to the one below it: a bare set with an
       operation becomes, in turn, a magma, a semigroup, a monoid, and finally a
       group, the central object of elementary algebra."
Id: "02a8aafc-c012-4d2a-92ed-b36b902d05a0"


SectionTitle: "Magmas"
Id: "399425a0-d94d-4548-953d-5dc82ac91e36"


Text: "The most basic structure is a set together with a single binary
       operation, with no further conditions imposed. This is a *magma*.
       Everything that follows is a magma with extra properties, so a magma
       marks the ground floor of the entire chapter."
Id: "b6b6f241-917e-4090-8269-5060f249b80c"


[\magma]
Declares: M ::= (X, *) is \set via X
specifies: * is \binary.operation:on{M}
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" M :<->: x_ is \magma.element:of{M}
  written: "x_? \in M?"
Documented:
. called: "magma"
. description: "A magma is a set $X$ together with a binary operation $*$ on it,
                subject to no further axioms. It is the weakest algebraic
                structure of this chapter, and every later structure is a magma
                with additional properties."
Id: "91ffd40c-72fa-4a93-83f3-80ceb1ae124a"


[\magma.element:of{M ::= (X, *)}]
Declares: x
when: M is \magma
extends: x "in" X
Enables:
. capability: x_ [*] y_ :=> x_ |M.*| y_
  written: "x_? *? y_?"
Documented:
. called: "element of magma $M?$"
. description: "An element of a magma $M$ is simply a member of its underlying
                set. Naming it as its own notion lets the operation of $M$ be
                written directly between two of its elements as $x * y$."
Id: "d9405849-68a5-4721-9090-42eb0b9775d2"


Text: "A quasigroup is a magma in which the operation can always be undone on
       either side: each of the equations $a * x = b$ and $y * a = b$ has a
       unique solution. This is the abstract form of the demand that division be
       possible, and it is where the study of invertibility begins."
Id: "55b0749b-4e2f-4112-a5be-c20a9879d6a3"


[\quasigroup]
Declares: Q ::= (X, *) is \magma via (X, *)
satisfies:
. forAll: a, b "in" Q
  then:
  . existsUnique: x, y "in" Q
    suchThat:
    . a * x = b
    . y * a = b
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" Q :<->: x_ is \quasigroup.element:of{Q}
  written: "x_? \in Q?"
Documented:
. called: "quasigroup"
. description: "A quasigroup is a magma in which left and right division are
                always possible: for all $a$ and $b$ the equations $a * x = b$
                and $y * a = b$ each have a unique solution. It captures
                invertibility without requiring associativity or an identity."
Id: "49582242-a047-48ae-aced-77654d9a48aa"


[\quasigroup.element:of{Q ::= (X, *)}]
Declares: x
when: Q is \quasigroup
extends: x is \magma.element:of{Q}
Documented:
. called: "element of quasigroup $Q?$"
Id: "2f5a9f56-286c-4703-a953-316fa32e57dc"


SectionTitle: "Semigroups and Monoids"
Id: "57daae70-4d40-44bf-9640-46c26d8a4898"


Text: "Imposing associativity on a magma yields a *semigroup*. Associativity is
       what lets a product of several elements be written without parentheses,
       and it is assumed by nearly every structure in mathematics that is called
       an algebra."
Id: "e077a5db-c372-4759-a880-d74270032560"


[\semigroup]
Declares: S ::= (X, *) is \magma via (X, *)
specifies: * is \(associative)::binary.operation:on{X}
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" S :<->: x_ is \semigroup.element:of{S}
  written: "x_? \in S?"
Documented:
. called: "semigroup"
. description: "A semigroup is a magma whose operation is associative, so that
                $(a * b) * c = a * (b * c)$ for all elements. Associativity
                makes repeated products unambiguous, so one may write
                $a * b * c$ without parentheses."
Id: "6ec2faf5-7d2c-4918-a559-6415b94f2b1f"


[\semigroup.element:of{S ::= (X, *)}]
Declares: x
when: S is \semigroup
extends: x is \magma.element:of{S}
Documented:
. called: "element of semigroup $S?$"
Id: "5ce95378-5760-4728-b0d2-334848109a32"


Text: "A *monoid* is a semigroup with an identity element -- a distinguished
       element that leaves every element unchanged when combined with it. The
       identity is the algebraic counterpart of the number $1$ under
       multiplication, or $0$ under addition."
Id: "c647adb5-0f98-4f7d-ae6a-8ff2229e3437"


[\monoid]
Declares: M ::= (X, *, e) is \semigroup via (X, *)
specifies: e "in" X
satisfies:
. forAll: x "in" M
  then:
  . x * e = x
  . e * x = x
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" M :<->: x is \monoid.element:of{M}
  written: "x_? \in M?"
Documented:
. called: "monoid"
. description: "A monoid is a semigroup possessing an identity element $e$, one
                that satisfies $x * e = x = e * x$ for every $x$. The identity
                is unique, and it plays the role that $0$ plays for addition or
                $1$ for multiplication."
Id: "034bf6ff-ba6e-4fac-b47e-65cc85d15642"


[\monoid.element:of{M}]
Declares: x
when: M is \monoid
extends: x is \semigroup.element:of{M}
Documented:
. called: "element of monoid $M?$"
Id: "fee7d2ca-5ebc-48a6-8eed-06d623b5520f"
